# Line endings in the WORKING TREE, not just in the index.
#
# Without this file a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true (Git for Windows'
# default) materialises CRLF sources, and this repository's gates read their own
# source text: internal/repohygiene, internal/doclint, internal/archguard and a
# dozen reachability checks in internal/mcpserver and cmd/server all parse or
# string-match Go files. Those matchers hard-code "\n", so on such a host they
# false-alarm on properties that hold — issue #163 measured
# TestSearchResultRendersTheCorrectionMark failing at "could not bound the
# searchHitView literal" over a literal that was there and correct.
#
# `eol=lf` is what fixes the class rather than one matcher. `text=auto` alone
# normalises the INDEX and still hands Windows a CRLF working tree, which is
# exactly the half that breaks: every gate reads the file on disk.
#
# Shell scripts are here for a harder reason than tidiness. The kit's hooks are
# bash, and a CRLF shebang line makes the interpreter path end in \r — the script
# then fails to execute with a message naming a binary that does not exist,
# which is a confusing failure a long way from its cause.
*.go    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf

# Binaries and archives must never be touched. Listing them is not paranoia:
# `text=auto` guesses, and a guess that goes wrong here corrupts the file
# silently rather than failing.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.wasm  binary
